<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faccetta nera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">April <a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Faccetta nera&quot; (&quot;Little black face&quot;) was written by the Roman poet Renato Micheli, with music by Mario Ruccione, in April 1935, as the Italian army prepared to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Fascist propaganda was then publishing stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Ethiopia, and it presented the coming invasion not as a war of conquest but as a war of liberation to abolish slavery; these stories served in part to justify the conquest.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The march is sung by an Italian Blackshirt soldier during the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italo-ethiopian-war">Second Italo-Ethiopian War</a>. He tells a beautiful young enslaved Abyssinian girl that she will be freed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and ruled by a new regime, and invites her to parade with the fascist Blackshirts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where she is promised a new and better life. The song is said to have been inspired by a young Abyssinian girl whom Italian troops found at the beginning of the invasion. Slavery in Ethiopia is its main theme, in keeping with the propaganda that presented the war as one of liberation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the invasion the song was hugely popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and stirred national fervour. Its implicitly erotic content soon embarrassed the Fascist government. Under the occupation, Ethiopian women lived with Italian men in a system of concubinage known as madamismo, but from May 1936 the regime passed laws forbidding cohabitation and marriage between Italians and the native peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian colonial empire</a>, which culminated in the Italian racial laws of 1938. The authorities considered banning the song, and they removed every picture postcard showing Abyssinian women from the shop windows of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>The song has since been heard in films. Martin Scorsese used it among the many Italian songs of Mean Streets (1973), and it is heard prominently in Francesco Rosi's Christ Stopped at Eboli, in a scene where Blackshirts prepare to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Faccetta nera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Faccetta nera</b>&quot; (&quot;Little black face&quot;) was written by the Roman poet Renato Micheli, with music by Mario Ruccione, in April 1935, as the Italian army prepared to invade Ethiopia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera</a></li></ul></section>
